A||||10||Spot||Flex additive in paint||||5||
Z||000000||Spot||03-19-2001||08:25 AM||||Has anyone used the flex additive on the entire car??? If you know of any known issues please assist. Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||03-19-2001||09:22 AM||||I do the whole car with the flex. No problem, just a little more expense-saves time though on overall jobs.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||03-19-2001||09:24 AM||||I dont ever use it to start with. I dont know if itll make any problems on the plastic, probably not. But it will add several hundred dollars to your paint bill. That stuff has got to be $40 @ pint by now, I havent bought it since 1970.||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||03-19-2001||10:14 AM||||Flex-agent is only added to paint for the cars re-assembly.. after a single 20minute bake at 140* it sweats it's way out.. kinda like the oils in polyurathane bumpers.. you have to sweat them before you paint them. DOn't waste you time and $... I worked at the Sikkens Training facility for over a Year as an instructor. if you have a clumsy non caring body man installing the parts.. then use a flex agent.. if your doing it.. save the $ for more clear and a pearl. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||03-19-2001||10:33 AM||||1FST2M6 - <P>Exactly what our paint supply shop said.  They said it only lasts about a half hour, and is used only for reassmbly.<P>Otherwise, think of how much the paint flexes already.  Push on your fascias and fenders.  Does the paint break or chip?  You should have no trouble at all leaving out the additive.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||03-19-2001||12:26 PM||||finally some more who agree with me. also think about this fact too. Why would you think your paint had to have flex agent in it to make it stay on your facia, the primer under it doesnt have any. Ive never heard of anyone ever trying to mix flex agent in primer surfacer.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||03-19-2001||08:21 PM||||Hey,<BR> I'm sure you guys a correct, but....<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Push on your fascias and fenders. Does the paint break or chip?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Well....Yes it does. I put a nice spider web in the rear fascia of my '87 GT with my knee when changing the engine. It's still the factory paint/clear. I also painted a coupe with cheap Sherwin Willams acrylic enamel a few years ago. It still looks OK but it has cracks in the paint wherever it's been bumped. The yellow GT was Sikkens, had lots of cracks in the fascias. I think the quality and age of the paint has a lot to do with it.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroJoe||03-19-2001||09:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR> <B>I also painted a coupe with cheap Sherwin Willams acrylic enamel a few years ago.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sherwin Williams?!?  I never knew they made automotive grade paint, unless you just used spray paint.  Makes a <B>huge</B> difference.<P>rogergarrison has a great point:  Flex additive is never used in primers and adhesion coats, and that is the only thing the paint is sticking to.<P>I did not crack any paint on my front fascia when I took it off, and that involves about as much flexing as it will ever get.<BR><P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||03-20-2001||07:10 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Sherwin Williams?!? I never knew they made automotive grade paint<HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <BR>Yep, they have a full line of automotive paints. My brother worked at a Ryder truck service center painting trucks. That's all they used. In fact that Fiero was Ryder yellow! ||||reg||1
Z||000009||1FST2M6||03-20-2001||08:26 AM||||even with a flex agent you would have still split the paint on your facia. the agent is only in the paint for a very short time... rarly more than 24hours. <P>Sherwin coatings are a great paint to work with... easy to apply and very forgiving! better than an Acme (not just in cartoons) or Permatex.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||03-20-2001||11:55 AM||||a lot of times its just the plastic bumper itself aging. the cracks in it will show thru the paint. If thats the case, no amount of sanding or filling/primer will cure it. It will always keep coming back sooner or later. Only permanent solution is replacing the part with a new one. Corvettes have had a problem with both facias drying out and cracking thru the paint. Some aftermarket vette parts houses are now making fiberglass replacements to eliminate the problem.||||reg||1
